      "content": "<h3>How LinkedHashMap works inside</h3>\n<p>It adds one doubly linked list on top of HashMap's buckets.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Entries are <code>LinkedHashMap.Entry&lt;K,V&gt;</code>: a HashMap node plus <code>before</code> and <code>after</code> references. The map keeps <code>head</code> (eldest) and <code>tail</code> (youngest).</li>\n<li>HashMap exposes three hooks that LinkedHashMap overrides: <code>afterNodeInsertion</code> (calls <code>removeEldestEntry</code>), <code>afterNodeAccess</code> (moves the entry to the tail in access-order mode) and <code>afterNodeRemoval</code> (unlinks the entry).</li>\n<li>Lookups still use the hash table, so <code>get</code>, <code>put</code> and <code>remove</code> stay O(1).</li>\n<li>Iteration follows <code>after</code> links: O(size), independent of capacity.</li>\n<li>Memory: two extra references (8 bytes with compressed pointers) per entry compared with HashMap.</li>\n<li>In access-order mode <code>get</code> relinks the entry, so it changes <code>modCount</code> and is <strong>not safe</strong> for concurrent readers.</li>\n</ul>"
